            <ArticleTitle>Thermography--its current diagnostic status in musculoskeletal medicine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>441-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the currently perceived status of thermography in the diagnosis of musculoskeletal disorders.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Medical and legal journals published from 1956 onwards; report of the United States Office of Health Technology Assessment and personal communication with the author of that report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY SELECTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Confined to application of thermography to musculoskeletal and neurological medicine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA EXTRACTION AND SYNTHESIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Weighted towards prospective and controlled studies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Little evidence exists of any application of thermography in which it is unequivocally superior to conventional diagnostic imaging methods.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
